---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PrimeNumberChecker from '@/components/tools/math/PrimeNumberChecker.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="prime-number-checker">
  <PrimeNumberChecker client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use it</h2>
    <p>
      The first tab tests a single number: it tells you whether the number is prime, shows the prime
      factorisation if it is not, counts the divisors, lists them for smaller inputs, and finds the nearest
      prime on either side. The second tab sieves an interval and lists every prime it contains.
    </p>

    <h2>What makes a number prime</h2>
    <p>
      A prime is a whole number greater than 1 whose only positive divisors are 1 and itself. 7 is prime;
      9 is not, because 3 divides it. Numbers greater than 1 that are not prime are called composite.
    </p>
    <p>
      1 is deliberately excluded. It has only one positive divisor, and if it counted as prime then unique
      factorisation would break: 6 could be written as 2 × 3, or 1 × 2 × 3, or 1 × 1 × 2 × 3. Mathematicians
      call 1 a unit. Zero and negative numbers are outside the definition entirely.
    </p>

    <h2>How the primality test works</h2>
    <p>
      A composite number n always has a factor no larger than √n, because if both factors were bigger their
      product would exceed n. So trial division only has to test primes up to the square root. For 9,973 that
      means up to 99, not up to 9,972.
    </p>
    <p>
      For large numbers even that is slow, so this tool uses the Miller–Rabin test. It asks whether n behaves
      the way a prime must behave under modular exponentiation for a set of chosen witnesses. With the first
      thirteen primes as witnesses, the test is proven exact for all values below 3.3 × 10²⁴. Above that it
      uses 33 witnesses and reports a probable prime. A 300-digit number gives an answer at once.
    </p>
    <p>
      Full factorisation is a harder problem than primality testing. Up to 10¹⁵ this tool uses trial division
      with a 6k ± 1 wheel. Above that, parallel WebAssembly workers run the elliptic curve
      method, which finds factors of up to about 35 digits. Two very large prime factors can stay out of reach.
      RSA encryption is built on exactly that difference in difficulty.
    </p>

    <h2>Reading the prime factorisation</h2>
    <p>
      Every integer above 1 factors into primes in exactly one way, ignoring order. That is the fundamental
      theorem of arithmetic. The tool writes it in prime-power form, so 360 = 2³ × 3² × 5.
    </p>
    <p>
      The exponents also give the divisor count without listing anything: multiply each exponent plus one
      together. For 360 that is (3+1) × (2+1) × (1+1) = 24 divisors, because each prime can appear anywhere
      from zero times up to its exponent, independently of the others.
    </p>

    <h2>The sieve of Eratosthenes</h2>
    <p>
      To list every prime in a range, the range generator uses a segmented sieve. It first finds all primes up
      to √(upper bound) by an ordinary sieve, then walks the requested interval in blocks, crossing off the
      multiples of each of those base primes. Whatever is left standing is prime. This is far faster than
      testing each number individually and uses memory proportional to the block size rather than the range.
    </p>
    <p>
      Alongside the list you get the count, the density, the number of twin-prime pairs (primes two apart, such
      as 11 and 13) and the largest gap in the interval.
    </p>

    <h2>Prime facts worth having</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Range</th><th scope="col">Primes in it</th><th scope="col">Density</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1 – 10</td><td>4</td><td>40%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1 – 100</td><td>25</td><td>25%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1 – 1,000</td><td>168</td><td>16.8%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1 – 10,000</td><td>1,229</td><td>12.29%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1 – 100,000</td><td>9,592</td><td>9.59%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1 – 1,000,000</td><td>78,498</td><td>7.85%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The thinning out is not random: the prime number theorem says the count below n is approximately
      n ÷ ln(n), which predicts 72,382 below a million, within 8% of the true 78,498. Euclid proved over two
      thousand years ago that the primes never run out, so there is no largest prime.
    </p>

    <h2>Numbers that look prime but are not</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>91 = 7 × 13: the classic trap, because it passes the divisibility tests for 2, 3 and 5.</li>
      <li>561 = 3 × 11 × 17: the smallest Carmichael number, which fools the naive Fermat primality test for every base coprime to it. Miller–Rabin is not fooled.</li>
      <li>2¹¹ − 1 = 2047 = 23 × 89: a Mersenne number with a prime exponent that is still composite.</li>
      <li>1: excluded by definition, as above.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      All the arithmetic, including the big-integer Miller–Rabin test and the sieve, runs inside your browser.
      Nothing you enter is sent anywhere, which also means the page keeps working offline.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
